like, sometimes I've known controllers that restart nodes.

667
00:45:41,480 --> 00:45:46,630
Kind of crazy, but there's use cases for it, right?

668
00:45:46,630 --> 00:45:52,031
Back in the day, I know folks over at Comcast were mounting two different video cards to a. node,

669
00:45:52,031 --> 00:45:56,070
and it would require a restart at times cuz this is actually a physical video card.

670
00:45:56,080 --> 00:46:01,870
When they're streaming and encrypting movies, you would be talking to an actual video card.

671
00:46:01,870 --> 00:46:03,550
that would be streaming you that movie.

672
00:46:03,550 --> 00:46:10,470
I'm sure it's changed a bit over the years but, you know, restarting, there are use cases.

673
00:46:10,470 --> 00:46:14,630
That's actually one of the Linux level permissions, whether you have permission to restart Linux.

674
00:46:14,630 --> 00:46:23,071
So, again, you're able to do that fine-grained type specification within a pod on what type of permissions. it has.

675
00:46:23,071 --> 00:46:24,870
And goes back to don't run as root within a container.

676
00:46:24,880 --> 00:46:29,710
But, that That's both pod configurations as well as container configurations.

677
00:46:29,710 --> 00:46:34,590
So, don't, you know, and there's a lot of people.

678
00:46:34,590 --> 00:46:36,310
that are a lot better experts than I am.

679
00:46:36,310 --> 00:46:39,870
when it comes to container level security because that's such a broad topic now.

680
00:46:39,870 --> 00:46:44,470
But, there's just some basics, you know, as well with container level security.

681
00:46:44,470 --> 00:46:49,910
But, yeah, you're able to go through, do AppArmor pro profiles, SE Linux profiles.

682
00:46:49,910 --> 00:46:55,870
There's a lot of fine-tuning you can do to isolate your workloads.

683
00:46:55,870 --> 00:47:02,630
and specify the workloads that are given elevated permissions are controlled with only the elevated permissions they need.

684
00:47:02,640 --> 00:47:04,910
In the short amount that we have left,

685
00:47:04,920 --> 00:47:09,550
I'd like to do a couple quick hits on container security.

686
00:47:09,550 --> 00:47:15,430
We did already discuss some issues around containers, such as whether they contain known vulnerabilities,

687
00:47:15,430 --> 00:47:17,750
where you get them from, who can pull them down.

688
00:47:17,750 --> 00:47:24,349
One other issue I had in my outline is the idea of container escape into the host.

689
00:47:24,349 --> 00:47:29,591
How big of a problem is that and what can you do about it? Don't run on host network.

690
00:47:29,591 --> 00:47:31,710
So, if you're not on the host network,

691
00:47:31,710 --> 00:47:37,150
the capability to escape to the host reduces tremendously.

692
00:47:37,150 --> 00:47:41,550
because of how the networking and the virtualization almost quasi virtualization works.

693
00:47:41,550 --> 00:47:43,671
You know, the routing isn't there. So, that's it.

694
00:47:43,680 --> 00:47:49,430
Also, there's some container systems provide the capability of isolating the workloads.

695
00:47:49,430 --> 00:47:54,670
As well as if you have workloads, you know, you can isolate workloads to run on specific nodes.

696
00:47:54,670 --> 00:47:56,950
So, if you do get to the host,

697
00:47:56,950 --> 00:48:02,230
you don't like say you have workload A that cannot talk to workload B.

698
00:48:02,230 --> 00:48:04,870
Like, it just can't happen, right?

699
00:48:04,870 --> 00:48:07,270
So, don't put workload A on the same nodes that workload B is on.

700
00:48:07,280 --> 00:48:09,390
Pretty simple.

701
00:48:09,390 --> 00:48:14,231
I knew of a bank that every application had its own Kubernetes cluster. Really interesting pattern.

702
00:48:14,231 --> 00:48:18,070
You know, not a bad thing.

703
00:48:18,070 --> 00:48:22,349
If you need to isolate your workloads at such a low level, then possibly look at that.

704
00:48:22,359 --> 00:48:26,349
There's also Kubernetes virtualization within Kubernetes now,

705
00:48:26,349 --> 00:48:29,510
where you can run a Kubernetes cluster inside of Kubernetes cuz that's multi-homing.

706
00:48:29,510 --> 00:48:31,230
But, I've I've digressed.

707
00:48:31,230 --> 00:48:36,311
Let's go back to image level security and container security. Upgrade.

708
00:48:36,311 --> 00:48:40,830
Have the capability where you run CI and CD often.

709
00:48:40,830 --> 00:48:42,670
Have a set of base images.

710
00:48:42,670 --> 00:48:46,310
You know, if your shop works in Python,

711
00:48:46,310 --> 00:48:52,391
works in Go, works in Java, have base images for your developers. Have your golden Java image.

712
00:48:52,400 --> 00:48:54,150
Have your golden Python image.

713
00:48:54,150 --> 00:48:56,470
Have those screened on a regular basis.

714
00:48:56,470 --> 00:48:58,790
Have those upgraded on a regular basis.

715
00:48:58,790 --> 00:49:01,190
It's really important.

716
00:49:01,190 --> 00:49:04,550
People I go into large companies, and people are still not screening their images.

717
00:49:04,550 --> 00:49:10,349
They screen their operating systems, so goodness gracious, their nodes are being screened,

718
00:49:10,349 --> 00:49:12,150
but bash inside of an image isn't being screened.

719
00:49:12,150 --> 00:49:14,110
Also, don't install bash if you don't need it.

720
00:49:14,110 --> 00:49:18,750
Don't install whois if you don't need it, right?

721
00:49:18,750 --> 00:49:21,630
For instance, Golang's one of the neat languages.

722
00:49:21,630 --> 00:49:23,590
where you can compile and have it run on virtually nothing.

723
00:49:23,590 --> 00:49:29,030
The less binaries that are in a an image, use it.

724
00:49:29,030 --> 00:49:31,070
Use multi-layer images for your builds, right?

725
00:49:31,070 --> 00:49:34,590
So, you have a build image where you have your,

726
00:49:34,590 --> 00:49:40,950
you know, libraries that you your your shared build libraries that you need for Python dependencies.

727
00:49:40,950 --> 00:49:45,390
but in the next image that you're actually deploying in a production and using, don't have those libraries in it.

728
00:49:45,390 --> 00:49:47,631
Don't have get installed. Don't have curl installed.

729
00:49:47,640 --> 00:49:49,870
Don't have, you know, wget installed.

730
00:49:49,880 --> 00:49:51,990
Just don't, right?

731
00:49:51,990 --> 00:49:57,630
But sometimes you have images, you know, CI's probably the worst, right?

732
00:49:57,630 --> 00:49:59,390
Because CI images need to, for instance, talk to GitHub.

733
00:49:59,390 --> 00:50:01,750
They need to do XYZ.

734
00:50:01,750 --> 00:50:04,670
They need to do all these different things.

735
00:50:04,670 --> 00:50:08,990
So, be rigorous about maintaining your own images and and upgrades.

736
00:50:08,990 --> 00:50:11,710
Don't run images as root, as we mentioned.

737
00:50:11,710 --> 00:50:14,551
Don't allow your images to do a lot. It should be run application.

738
00:50:14,560 --> 00:50:20,390
That's it. If you, you know, run Python 3.12,

739
00:50:20,390 --> 00:50:22,670
when you have time, I can and make sure.

740
00:50:22,670 --> 00:50:26,830
that you have a backlog to upgrade to Python 3.13.

741
00:50:26,830 --> 00:50:29,150
You know, there's a reason that you want to keep upgrading.

742
00:50:29,150 --> 00:50:34,550
Also, have it simple as possible to redeploy your entire stack.

743
00:50:34,550 --> 00:50:36,790
It's good for DR, but it's also good.

744
00:50:36,790 --> 00:50:39,350
when there's a huge bug that comes out.

745
00:50:39,350 --> 00:50:44,071
that allows to remote execution and you run that library everywhere. Log for J comes to mind.

746
00:50:44,080 --> 00:50:50,630
You've got to be able to push a button and as quickly as possible redeploy your entire stack.

747
00:50:50,630 --> 00:50:52,470
Kubernetes is really good for allowing to you to do that.

748
00:50:52,470 --> 00:50:57,270
Don't get me wrong, but it's still, you know, down to other principles,

749
00:50:57,270 --> 00:50:59,430
which is your CI and CD system.

750
00:50:59,430 --> 00:51:01,310
Make sure that that's tuned in so you can do it.

751
00:51:01,310 --> 00:51:06,430
And the way you get to do that more often is is upgrading often, right?

752
00:51:06,430 --> 00:51:12,830
So, if you're upgrading regularly, you know you can redeploy your workloads regularly as well.

753
00:51:12,830 --> 00:51:17,510
We've hit on some pretty big mountains in the landscape of Kubernetes security, Chris.

754
00:51:17,510 --> 00:51:21,270
In uh time that we have left to wrap up, is there any parting words you'd like.

755
00:51:21,270 --> 00:51:28,430
the listeners to have of top three things to think about in securing your Kubernetes cluster?

